student_response:
Thank you for pointing that out – I see now that I was just reading off the y‑value at \(t=8\) instead of actually finding the slope of the curve at that point.

Since the problem gives us the tangent line drawn at \((8,51)\) and we can see that the same line also passes through the point \((0,40)\), we can use those two points to estimate the gradient (i.e. the instantaneous rate of change of velocity at \(t=8\) s).

The gradient is

\[
\text{gradient} \;=\; \frac{\Delta v}{\Delta t}
   \;=\; \frac{51-40}{8-0}
   \;=\; \frac{11}{8}
   \;=\; 1.375 .
\]

So the best estimate for the gradient when \(t=8\) s is **C. \(1.375\)**.

