student_response:
I’m sorry, I must have read the numbers wrong the first time.  

The line on the graph goes from the point \((0,\;£2)\) to the point \((5,\;£6)\).  
The cost per mile is just the slope of that line:

\[
\text{slope}=\frac{\text{change in cost}}{\text{change in distance}}
      =\frac{6-2}{5-0}
      =\frac{4}{5}
      =0.8\;\text{£ per mile}.
\]

So the cost per mile is **£0.80**, which corresponds to answer **D**.

